      "text": "In Being and Time, Heidegger argues that people are always already thrown into a world of involvements. We are always comporting ourselves in some manner, that is, doing things, and this doing is integral to the way the world shows up for us.° For Heidegger, the scientific explanation of world as the ensemble of quantifiable, objectively determinable facts that describe matter and forces arrayed within the infinite void of space accounts for only one possible cosmology. From a phenomenological perspective, the world is also our involvements, the ensemble of things, situations, and purposes that define the everyday. We do not, on this account, theoretically grasp the world that comes first. To pick a famous example, when I use a hammer to pound nails, I do not have a theory of hammers, or even a good definition of hammers, that underpins my picking a hammer up and using it against a backdrop of organizing purposes (the need to fix a broken board in the deck), know-how (this kind of wood, obtained at this location, shaped and fit in this way), and equipment (nails, boards, screws, reciprocating saw, and the troublesome deck). I need it, so I just pick it up and use it, and such usage fits within the range of available knowledge and accompanying equipment. This is so because I have already been in the\n\nworld for a while, during which time I have learned, from being with others and coping with the things of the world, the enculturated and practical knowledge that makes a world show up for me. But this showing up is not disinterested contemplation, or knowing that (for instance, I know that a computer possesses various qualities); rather, it is engaged comportment, or knowing how (for instance, I know how to use this computer and its programs to write, send e-mail, and so forth, and I live in a manner that makes such things necessary for me). But this background understanding and lived engagement reach further back than even such practical forms of skill and purpose. Our ability to pick out things and do things in the world-this is a tree, this is a rock, this is how to walk, this is how to talkalways work from an achieved tacit background of know-how and baseline intelligibility. Even prior to using a hammer, then, we are working from a sense of world that includes hammers, among other things. Language, too, works out of such a background. Heidegger states, \"Discoursing or talking is the way in which we articulate 'significantly' the intelligibility of Beingin-the-world\" (BT 204). This indicates that for Heidegger, at least for the early Heidegger of Being and Time, language as discoursing emerges from a background of holistic meanings and tacit coping that constitutes our world of involvements. It further shows that meaning already permeates the world around us, even ifit is not yet linguistic meaning.\n\nThis becomes clearer in a passage that is worth comparing to the previously quoted passage from Burke. Heidegger is discussing what it is that we hear when sounds arise:\n\nWhat we \"first\" hear is never noises or complexes of sounds, but the creaking waggon, the motor-cycle. We hear the column on the march, the north wind, the woodpecker tapping, the fire crackling .... It requires a very artificial and complicated frame of mind to \"hear\" a \"pure noise.\" The fact that motor-cycles and waggons are what we proximally hear is the phenomenal evidence that in every case Dasein, as Being-in-the-world, already dwells alongside what is ready-to-hand within-the-world; it certainly does not dwell proximally alongside \"sensations\"; nor would it first have to give shape to the swirl of sensations to provide the springboard from which the subject leaps off and finally arrives at a \"world.\" (BT 207)\n\nOne of Heidegger's main goals inBeing and Time and elsewhere is to dissolve the subject-object relationship that structures so much contemporary (and implicitly Cartesian) thinking. Instead of positing human subjects",
