      "text": "Ambient music, I am arguing, brings together at a practical level many of the thematic strands I have been discussing, including ambience's connection to rhetoricity. 20 It evokes the concreteness of place through various experiential registers, both for audience and composer, richly enough to constitute what I mean by situation (thought here in terms of Befindlichkeit), the affective, material disposition in which one finds oneself. Thus, the local environs are not background for music's emergence but, in countless ways, an active player in their own right: from the sourcing of found sound (sea bells, insects, or wind and rain); to the acoustic properties of the recording environment, whether \"natural\" (the previously mentioned Headley Grange for Led Zeppelin) or \"artificial\" (the drained, empty, electronically produced echo of Joy Division as engineered by the producer Martin Hannett on Unknown Pleasures); to the use of equipment in the composing process itself (turntables for DJs, amplifiers to produce feedback, or tape loops) (see Fuller 13-53); or even to the creation of \"impossible'' places realized through the artifice of sound (Eno'sAmbient 4: On Land and Eno and Hass ell's Fourth World, Vol. 1: Possible Musics). The latter point connects back to Clark's assertion about \"the ways that the body and the local environment are literally built into the processing loops that result in intelligent action\" (Being xii), except here, it is not always action but sometimes experience.\n\nLet me strengthen the connection here between rhetoric, music, and place. The world reveals itself in a musical way, but it does so in such fashion as to transform our experience of place itself. Ambient rhetoric, analogically, brings the world to us but in doing so transforms the disposition of our inhabitancy. This, as I have stated already, is my reworking of what rhetoric is: the world is revealed differently, at least potentially so, in a way that calls for some action. By implication, rhetoric from an ambient perspective can no longer be situated solely in human subjective performance.\n\nAmbient music sounds out the importance of place in conceiving not just rhetoric's performative work but also rhetoricity itself as worldly affectabilityprior to symbolicity.\n\nIndeed, the importance that ambient music attributes to place has wider implications about the power of place as a general and significant matter in its own right. Issues of space and place have gathered momentum in the twentieth century, in the work of Gaston Bachelard, Henri Lefebvre, Alfred North Whitehead, Michel Foucault. Edward Soja, and many more. Heidegger has also been important here, as is borne out especially in the work of Edward Casey, who has done much to put place back into theoretical dis-\n\ncussion (see The Fate of Place). As I discuss in chapter 1, on the chiira, place has since Aristotle largely been conceived categorically and transcendentally, so that place is assimilated to space (Casey x). Heidegger gives back to place its thick particularity; instead of being just another locatable site within a generalized spatial matrix, place becomes habitation. a material. unique location wherein people and things are situated (Casey 20; see also Walter). As Heidegger says, the ambient environs, the '\"round-about[.]' is not at all defined by a being-located-side-by-side-and-around-each-other and by geometrical relations, but rather it is the round-about of worldly dealings with it which are concerned about it, attend to it, and go around in it\" (0 79). Meaning, affect. and attachment become integral to place, and as ambient music also shows us, \"place is indeed an active source ofpresencing: within its close embrace, things get located and begin to happen\" (Casey 63). And here we can return full circle to my first, ancient example, the caves at Lascaux. Lascaux is a source of presencing involving the full range of early human existence, including its specific comportment toward aspects of the nonhuman. It is not just a location that happens to be the bearer of early aesthetic and ritualistic designs but a unique place that was integral to their emergence, a kind of window on how the world worlded for them (despite the fact that we may not know the actual details and import of this work). Our traditional sense of aesthetics, among other things, tends to sunder the \"art\" from the mundane materiality of the cave itself, but this occludes how the material environment is itself already part of what the \"art\" is. In discussing persuasion, we risk the same reductive move when we exclude the full range of ambient, material phenomena. When we want to examine, say, Howard Dean's 2004 Democratic nomination race \"I have a scream\" speech, we cannot divorce such discussion from the material environments in which the \"scream\" emerged, environments that were integral to how it was understood, from the auditorium for the live audience",
